    "model": "gemini-3.1-pro-preview",
    "generated": "2026-09-11",
    "what_it_is": "endo-Fenchol is a volatile monoterpenoid, a type of aromatic molecule that helps give certain plants their distinctive scents. As a close chemical relative of camphor, it contributes a fresh, earthy, and slightly pine-like aroma to the natural world. You might encounter it in the fragrance of certain herbs, spices, and even in the complex bouquet of some wines.",
    "found_in": "This compound is naturally found in fennel (Foeniculum vulgare) and the needles of the Norway spruce (Picea abies). It is also a component of the essential oils in ironwort (Sideritis), a plant often brewed into traditional herbal teas.",
    "why_researchers_care": "Researchers are drawn to endo-fenchol for its role as a fragrant volatile organic compound and its potential defensive properties in plants. They are actively exploring its capacity to act as an antioxidant and antimicrobial agent, with the strongest current evidence coming from human observational studies (Oxford level 2b). Scientists are also investigating how derivatives of this molecule might interact with viral entry processes, making it a fascinating subject in both flavor chemistry and early-stage drug discovery.",
